\title{Doppelg\"anger Phase-Lock:\\ A Complete Theory of Synchronization\\ for Identical Reactive Agents}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Two spatially separated but structurally identical deterministic agents observe the same
environmental stimulus stream. Under what conditions do they arrive, after finitely many
observations, at exactly the same internal state --- regardless of how differently they
were initialized? We develop a complete theory of this phenomenon, which we call
\emph{doppelg\"anger phase-lock}.

We prove a \textbf{Synchronization Theorem}: for a finite internal state space, the
existence of a universal locking stimulus word is equivalent to the mergeability of every
individual pair of states, with an explicit bound of $(|S|-1)L$ on the locking time when
pairs merge within $L$ stimuli, and an unconditional bound of $(|S|-1)|S|^2$. As a
corollary, phase-lockability is a decidable property of an agent design. We complement
this with three sharp boundary results: a \textbf{reversibility obstruction} showing that
phase-lock is impossible whenever every stimulus acts injectively (synchronization
requires dissipation); a counterexample on an infinite state space showing that pairwise
mergeability does not imply global phase-lock without finiteness; and a
\textbf{no-signalling theorem} proving that, in the product system, each agent's state
depends only on its own initial state and its own stimuli, so phase-lock is a shared-cause
correlation rather than a communication channel.

We then determine how typical phase-lock is. Locking words form a two-sided ideal of the
free stimulus monoid, from which a block-counting argument yields a failure fraction of at
most $(1-q^{-1})^m$ over $m$ blocks with $q=|I|^L$ block values, tending to zero.
Topologically, in the Cantor space of infinite stimulus streams, the lock set is always
open and --- for a phase-locking design --- dense, giving a \textbf{zero--one law}: the
lock set is either empty or open and dense with nowhere-dense complement. We show this is
sharp. We supply an analytic mechanism (uniform contraction with factor $k<1$ forces
exponential collapse of the doppelg\"anger gap, and quantization upgrades this to exact
locking uniformly in the stream) and prove it strictly stronger than the phenomenon. A
structural calculus establishes that phase-lock is preserved by parallel composition
(locking times add), by surjective simulation, and functorially by stimulus relabelling;
for monotone agents on a linear order we prove \textbf{order rigidity}, improving the
bound to $|S|^2$. Finally, certified exhaustive search establishes exact minimal
phase-lock times $4=(3-1)^2$ and $9=(4-1)^2$ for the three- and four-state
\v{C}ern\'y agents, situating the theory against the \v{C}ern\'y conjecture.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} synchronizing word, reset sequence, deterministic automaton,
\v{C}ern\'y conjecture, transition monoid, contraction mapping, zero--one law,
no-signalling.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The phenomenon}

Consider two copies of one machine, built to the same blueprint and separated in space,
with no communication link of any kind. Their initial internal configurations are unknown
and may differ arbitrarily. The environment then emits a stream of stimuli, and ---
crucially --- \emph{both copies observe the same stream}. Each reacts privately and
deterministically.

It can happen that after finitely many observations the two copies occupy \emph{exactly}
the same internal state, and that this holds regardless of their initial states. To an
outside observer, the two machines have spontaneously agreed. The suggestive framing is
``telepathic synchronization''; the mathematics dissolves the suggestion entirely, and
does so in a way that is much more interesting than the framing.

The formal content of the phenomenon is classical in one guise: a word that maps all
states of a deterministic automaton to a single state is a \emph{synchronizing word}, or
\emph{reset sequence}. What is developed here is a systematic theory of the phenomenon as
a statement about \emph{pairs of separated agents}, with attention paid equally to what
the phenomenon permits and to what it forbids.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Core algebra} (\S\ref{sec:algebra}): the stimulus monoid acts on the state
space; the locking words form a two-sided ideal; phase-lock is equivalent to the
transition monoid containing a constant (rank-one) element.
\item \textbf{Synchronization Theorem} (\S\ref{sec:sync}): finiteness converts pairwise
mergeability into global locking, with bounds $(|S|-1)L$ and unconditionally
$(|S|-1)|S|^2$.
\item \textbf{Boundaries} (\S\ref{sec:boundary}): reversibility obstruction; failure on
infinite state spaces; no-signalling and the indispensability of identical stimuli.
\item \textbf{Genericity} (\S\ref{sec:generic}): geometric decay of the failure fraction;
a topological zero--one law, with a sharpness witness.
\item \textbf{Analytic mechanism} (\S\ref{sec:contraction}): contraction implies
exponential and then exact phase-lock; the mechanism is strictly stronger than the
phenomenon.
\item \textbf{Structural calculus} (\S\ref{sec:structure}): parallel composition,
coarse-graining, relabelling; order rigidity and a quadratic bound.
\item \textbf{Effectivity and extremal data} (\S\ref{sec:effective}): decidability; exact
minimal locking times $4$ and $9$ for the three- and four-state \v{C}ern\'y agents.
\end{enumerate}

\section{The model}

\begin{definition}[Agent]
An \emph{agent} consists of a set $S$ of internal states, a set $I$ of environmental
stimuli, and a transition rule $\delta : S \times I \to S$. We write $\delta(s,i)$ for the
state entered from $s$ upon observing $i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Drive]
For a finite stimulus word $w = i_1 i_2 \cdots i_n \in I^{*}$ define
$\drive(w,\cdot) : S \to S$ by
\[
  \drive(\varepsilon, s) = s, \qquad \drive(i \cdot w, s) = \drive(w, \delta(s,i)).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Composition]\label{lem:comp}
For all $w, v \in I^{*}$ and $s \in S$, $\drive(wv, s) = \drive(v, \drive(w, s))$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from the fold characterization: folding over a concatenation is folding over the
first list and then over the second.
\end{proof}

For an infinite stimulus stream $x : \mathbb{N} \to I$ write $x\restr n$ for its
length-$n$ prefix.

\begin{definition}[Locking, mergeability, phase-lock]
A word $w \in I^{*}$ \emph{locks} $\delta$ if $\drive(w,s) = \drive(w,t)$ for all
$s,t \in S$. A pair $(s,t)$ is \emph{mergeable} if $\drive(w,s) = \drive(w,t)$ for some
$w$. The agent $\delta$ is \emph{phase-locking} if some word locks it.
\end{definition}

Two separated copies of $\delta$, in unknown states $s$ and $t$, both fed $w$, end in
states $\drive(w,s)$ and $\drive(w,t)$. A locking word forces these to agree for every
$(s,t)$.

\begin{proposition}[Constant characterization]\label{prop:const}
For $S$ nonempty, $w$ locks $\delta$ if and only if there is $c \in S$ with
$\drive(w,s) = c$ for all $s$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
If $w$ locks, fix $s_0$ and take $c = \drive(w,s_0)$. Conversely, constancy gives equality
of all pairs.
\end{proof}

\section{The core algebra of locking}\label{sec:algebra}

\subsection{Locking is absorbing, and forms an ideal}

\begin{lemma}[Suffix stability]\label{lem:suffix}
If $w$ locks $\delta$ then so does $wv$ for every $v$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\drive(wv,s) = \drive(v,\drive(w,s)) = \drive(v,\drive(w,t)) = \drive(wv,t)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Prefix stability]\label{lem:prefix}
If $w$ locks $\delta$ then so does $uw$ for every $u$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\drive(uw,s) = \drive(w,\drive(u,s))$, and $w$ locks all pairs, in particular
$(\drive(u,s),\drive(u,t))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ideal Theorem]\label{thm:ideal}
The set of locking words is a two-sided ideal of the free monoid $I^{*}$: if $w$ locks,
then $uwv$ locks for all $u,v \in I^{*}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Lemmas \ref{lem:suffix} and \ref{lem:prefix}.
\end{proof}

Operationally: \emph{once telepathy is possible, no amount of extra environmental noise,
before or after, can destroy it.}

\begin{corollary}[Absorption]\label{cor:absorb}
If $\drive(w,s)=\drive(w,t)$ then $\drive(wv,s)=\drive(wv,t)$ for all $v$: the diagonal of
$S\times S$ is invariant under the common dynamics.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Block locking]\label{lem:block}
If a stimulus stream is cut into blocks $b_1,\dots,b_m$ and some block $b_j$ locks, then
the concatenation $b_1 b_2 \cdots b_m$ locks.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on the block list, applying Lemma \ref{lem:prefix} to the prefix and Lemma
\ref{lem:suffix} to the suffix of the locking block.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The transition monoid}

Because $\drive$ composes contravariantly (Lemma \ref{lem:comp}), the assignment
$w \mapsto \drive(w,\cdot)$ is a monoid homomorphism
$T_\delta : I^{*} \to \End(S)^{\mathrm{op}}$ into the \emph{opposite} endomorphism monoid.
Its image is the \emph{transition monoid} of the agent.

\begin{theorem}[Rank-one characterization]\label{thm:rankone}
For $S$ nonempty, $\delta$ is phase-locking if and only if the transition monoid contains
a constant map, i.e.\ an element of rank one.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A locking word $w$ has $\drive(w,\cdot) = \mathrm{const}_c$ by Proposition
\ref{prop:const}, and this map lies in the image of $T_\delta$. Conversely, if
$\drive(w,\cdot)$ is constant then $w$ locks.
\end{proof}

\section{The Synchronization Theorem}\label{sec:sync}

Throughout this section $S$ is finite.

\subsection{Rank}

\begin{definition}[Rank]
The \emph{rank} of a word $w$ is
$\rank(w) := \bigl|\{\drive(w,s) : s \in S\}\bigr|$, the number of internal states still
distinguishable after $w$ has been observed. Note $\rank(\varepsilon) = |S|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Rank is antitone]\label{thm:rank}
$\rank(wv) \le \rank(w)$ for all $w, v$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The image of $\drive(wv,\cdot)$ is the image under $\drive(v,\cdot)$ of the image of
$\drive(w,\cdot)$, by Lemma \ref{lem:comp}; images do not grow under further maps.
\end{proof}

Rank is a monotone potential: observing stimuli only ever destroys information about the
initial state. This is the precise sense in which phase-lock is \emph{dissipative}.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:rank1}
For $S$ nonempty finite, $w$ locks $\delta$ if and only if $\rank(w) = 1$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Greedy collapse}

\begin{lemma}[A collision strictly drops cardinality]\label{lem:collision}
Let $f : S \to S$, let $A \subseteq S$ be finite, and suppose $s \ne t$ both lie in $A$
with $f(s)=f(t)$. Then $|f(A)| < |A|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$f(A) = f(A \setminus \{s\})$, since the value $f(s)$ is attained at $t \in A\setminus\{s\}$.
Hence $|f(A)| \le |A| - 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Greedy collapse]\label{lem:greedy}
Suppose every pair of states is mergeable by a word of length at most $L$. Then for every
nonempty finite $A \subseteq S$ there is a word $w$ with $|w| \le (|A|-1)L$ and
$|\{\drive(w,a) : a \in A\}| = 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Strong induction on $|A|$. If $|A| = 1$ take $w = \varepsilon$. Otherwise pick distinct
$s,t \in A$ and a merging word $v$ with $|v| \le L$. Set $B := \drive(v, A)$; by Lemma
\ref{lem:collision}, $|B| < |A|$ and $B \ne \varnothing$. By induction there is $u$ with
$|u| \le (|B|-1)L$ collapsing $B$ to a point. Then $vu$ collapses $A$, and
$|vu| \le L + (|B|-1)L = |B|L \le (|A|-1)L$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Synchronization Theorem, quantitative form]\label{thm:sync}
Let $S$ be finite and nonempty and suppose every pair of internal states is mergeable by a
word of length at most $L$. Then there is a single word $w$ with $|w| \le (|S|-1)L$ that
locks $\delta$: the two separated doppelg\"angers phase-lock from any pair of initial
states.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma \ref{lem:greedy} with $A = S$; a singleton image is exactly locking by
Proposition \ref{prop:rank1}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pairwise telepathy is global telepathy]\label{thm:pairwise}
For a finite nonempty state space, $\delta$ is phase-locking if and only if every pair
$(s,t)$ of states is mergeable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) A locking word merges every pair. ($\Leftarrow$) Choose for each pair a
merging word and let $L$ be the maximum of their lengths --- finite, because $S\times S$
is finite. Apply Theorem \ref{thm:sync}.
\end{proof}

A local, pairwise ability bootstraps into a global, uniform one. Finiteness is what makes
the bootstrap legitimate, and \S\ref{sec:boundary} shows it cannot be dropped.

\subsection{Removing the parameter $L$}

\begin{lemma}[Pigeonhole in the pair system]\label{lem:pigeon}
Let $S$ be finite. If $\drive(w,s) = \drive(w,t)$ and $|w| > |S|^2$, then there is a
strictly shorter $v$ with $\drive(v,s) = \drive(v,t)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Consider $k \mapsto (\drive(w\restr k, s), \drive(w\restr k, t))$ for $0 \le k \le |w|$, a
map from a set of size $|w|+1 > |S|^2$ into $S\times S$. Two indices $a<b$ give the same
pair. Excise the loop: $v := (w\restr a)\,(w_{\ge b})$ has $|v| < |w|$, and driving $s$ or
$t$ by $v$ gives the same result as driving by $w$, since the pair after $w\restr a$
agrees coordinatewise with the pair after $w\restr b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Short merges]\label{cor:short}
Every mergeable pair merges within $|S|^2$ stimuli.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Unconditional phase-lock time]\label{thm:cubic}
If a finite nonempty agent phase-locks at all, it does so within $(|S|-1)|S|^{2}$ shared
stimuli.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem \ref{thm:pairwise} every pair is mergeable; by Corollary \ref{cor:short} within
$L = |S|^2$; apply Theorem \ref{thm:sync}.
\end{proof}

\section{Boundaries: what phase-lock cannot do}\label{sec:boundary}

\subsection{Reversibility obstruction: synchronization requires dissipation}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:inj}
If every stimulus occurring in $w$ acts injectively on $S$, then $\drive(w,\cdot)$ is
injective.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Reversibility obstruction]\label{thm:rev}
Suppose every stimulus $i \in I$ acts injectively, i.e.\ $s \mapsto \delta(s,i)$ is
injective. If $S$ has at least two distinct elements, then $\delta$ is \textbf{not}
phase-locking.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $w$ locks and $s \ne t$. By Lemma \ref{lem:inj}, $\drive(w,\cdot)$ is injective,
and $\drive(w,s) = \drive(w,t)$ forces $s=t$.
\end{proof}

Injectivity per stimulus is the discrete analogue of reversible, information-preserving
dynamics. Hence: \emph{telepathic synchronization requires dissipative internal dynamics}.

\begin{example}[Parity agent]
$S = \{0,1\}$, one stimulus, $\delta(s,\ast) = \lnot s$. Every stimulus is a bijection, so
two parity doppelg\"angers started out of phase remain out of phase forever.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Copy agent]\label{ex:copy}
$S = \{0,1\} = I$, $\delta(s,i) = i$: the agent overwrites its memory with what it
observes. Any single stimulus is a locking word.
\end{example}

\subsection{Finiteness is indispensable}

\begin{definition}[Countdown agent]
$S = \mathbb{N}$, one stimulus, $\delta(s,\ast) = s - 1$ (truncated at $0$).
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\drive(w, s) = s - |w|$, truncated at $0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proposition}
Every pair of countdown states is mergeable: for $s,t$, the word of length $\max(s,t)$
drives both to $0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[No global lock]\label{thm:countdown}
The countdown agent is not phase-locking. Consequently, pairwise mergeability does not
imply phase-lock on an infinite state space, and Theorem \ref{thm:pairwise} genuinely
requires finiteness.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For any candidate $w$ with $n := |w|$, $\drive(w, n+1) = 1 \ne 0 = \drive(w, 0)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{No signalling, and the necessity of identical stimuli}

Model the two separated agents as a single product system in which each copy receives its
\emph{own} local stimulus:
\[
  \Delta\bigl((p_1,p_2),(q_1,q_2)\bigr) = \bigl(\delta(p_1,q_1),\ \delta(p_2,q_2)\bigr).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Locality/factorization]\label{thm:factor}
For any joint word $W \in (I\times I)^{*}$ and any $(s,t)$,
\[
  \drive_\Delta(W, (s,t)) = \bigl(\drive_\delta(\pi_1 W, s),\ \drive_\delta(\pi_2 W, t)\bigr),
\]
where $\pi_1 W,\pi_2 W$ are the componentwise projections.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[No-signalling]\label{thm:nosig}
Let $W, W'$ be joint stimulus words with $\pi_2 W = \pi_2 W'$, and let $s, s', t$ be
states. Then
\[
  \bigl(\drive_\Delta(W, (s,t))\bigr)_2 = \bigl(\drive_\Delta(W', (s',t))\bigr)_2 .
\]
Agent 2's internal state depends only on agent 2's own initial state and its own stimulus
stream; neither agent 1's initial state nor agent 1's stimuli have any influence.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem \ref{thm:factor}.
\end{proof}

Phase-lock is therefore \emph{not a channel}. The correlation is a \emph{shared-cause}
correlation: one common signal, two identical mechanisms, one common outcome.

\begin{theorem}[Diagonal characterization]
A word $w$ locks $\delta$ if and only if the diagonal joint word
$(w_1,w_1)(w_2,w_2)\cdots$ steers the product system onto the diagonal of $S\times S$ from
every initial configuration.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Identical stimuli are indispensable]\label{thm:distinct}
Two copy agents (Example \ref{ex:copy}) started at $(1,0)$ and driven by the constant
differing joint stimulus $(1,0)$ repeated $n$ times are in distinct states for every $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem \ref{thm:factor} their states are $\drive(1^n,1)=1$ and $\drive(0^n,0)=0$.
\end{proof}

\section{How typical is phase-lock?}\label{sec:generic}

\subsection{Geometric decay of the failure fraction}

Let $u$ be a locking word of length $L$ and cut a stimulus stream into $m$ consecutive
blocks of length $L$. By Lemma \ref{lem:block}, the stream locks as soon as one block
equals $u$.

\begin{theorem}[Counting the failures]\label{thm:count}
Let $I$ be finite. Among the $(|I|^L)^m$ block sequences of length $Lm$, at most
$(|I|^L - 1)^{m}$ fail to lock.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The failing sequences inject into $\prod_{j=1}^m (I^L \setminus \{u\})$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric decay]\label{thm:decay}
Writing $q := |I|^L$, the fraction of length-$Lm$ stimulus streams that fail to lock is at
most $\left(1 - 1/q\right)^{m}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Blind driving locks almost surely]
If $q \ge 2$, the failure fraction tends to $0$ as $m \to \infty$. Under uniform blind
environmental driving, doppelg\"anger phase-lock is an asymptotically probability-one
event.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{A topological zero--one law}

Equip $I^{\mathbb{N}}$ with the product topology of the discrete alphabet (Cantor
topology) and define the \emph{lock set}
\[
  \mathcal{L}(\delta) := \{ x \in I^{\mathbb{N}} :
      \text{some finite prefix } x\restr n \text{ locks } \delta \}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Openness]\label{thm:open}
$\mathcal{L}(\delta)$ is open.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $x\restr n$ locks, every stream agreeing with $x$ on the first $n$ coordinates has the
same locking prefix; that agreement set is a basic open cylinder around $x$ inside
$\mathcal{L}(\delta)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Density]\label{thm:dense}
If $\delta$ is phase-locking, then $\mathcal{L}(\delta)$ is dense.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $U$ be nonempty open, containing a basic cylinder constraining coordinates in a finite
set $F$. Let $N > \max F$, let $x$ realize the constraint, and let $w$ be a locking word.
Splice: follow $x$ for $N$ steps, then emit $w$. The result lies in $U$ because the
coordinates in $F$ are untouched, and its prefix of length $N+|w|$ is $(x\restr N)w$,
which locks by Lemma \ref{lem:prefix}.
\end{proof}

\emph{No experiment can be spoiled beyond repair}: however the environment has behaved on
a finite record, a continuation that phase-locks is always available.

\begin{theorem}[Zero--one law]\label{thm:zeroone}
For $I$ nonempty, exactly one of the following holds: $\mathcal{L}(\delta) = \varnothing$,
which happens precisely when $\delta$ is not phase-locking; or $\mathcal{L}(\delta)$ is
open and dense, with nowhere-dense complement.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Sharpness}

\begin{definition}[\v{C}ern\'y agent, $n=3$]
$S = \{0,1,2\}$, $I = \{a,b\}$, with $a$ the rotation $s \mapsto s+1 \bmod 3$ and $b$ the
collapse $0 \mapsto 1$, $s \mapsto s$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[A non-locking stream for a locking agent]\label{thm:nonlock}
The constant stream $aaa\cdots$ lies outside $\mathcal{L}$ for the three-state \v{C}ern\'y
agent, even though that agent is phase-locking.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every prefix is a power of $a$, which acts bijectively; by Lemma \ref{lem:inj} no prefix
can merge the distinct states $0$ and $1$. Meanwhile $baab$ locks
(Theorem \ref{thm:cerny3}).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
For the three-state \v{C}ern\'y agent, $\mathcal{L}$ is open and dense, but neither closed
nor equal to the whole stream space.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Openness and density are Theorems \ref{thm:open}, \ref{thm:dense}; properness is Theorem
\ref{thm:nonlock}. A dense proper subset cannot be closed.
\end{proof}

\section{An analytic mechanism: contraction}\label{sec:contraction}

Suppose $S$ carries a (pseudo)metric $d$ and every stimulus is a uniform $k$-contraction:
$d(\delta(s,i), \delta(t,i)) \le k\, d(s,t)$ for all $i,s,t$, with $0 \le k < 1$.

\begin{theorem}[Exponential collapse of the doppelg\"anger gap]\label{thm:contract}
For every word $w$,
$d\bigl(\drive(w,s), \drive(w,t)\bigr) \le k^{|w|}\, d(s,t)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $w$, applying the contraction hypothesis at each step.
\end{proof}

The bound does not depend on \emph{which} stimuli occurred: the rate is a property of the
agent, uniform over all environments.

\begin{corollary}[Asymptotic phase-lock]
Along every infinite stimulus stream $x$ and for all $s,t$,
$d(\drive(x\restr n, s), \drive(x\restr n, t)) \to 0$ as $n\to\infty$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Quantization upgrades approximate to exact]\label{thm:quant}
Suppose additionally that distinct states are separated, $d(s,t) \ge \varepsilon > 0$ for
$s\ne t$, and the space is bounded, $d(s,t) \le D$. Then there is $N$, depending only on
$k,\varepsilon,D$, such that \emph{every} word of length $\ge N$ locks $\delta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose $N$ with $k^N < \varepsilon/(D+1)$. If $|w| \ge N$ and
$\drive(w,s) \ne \drive(w,t)$ then
$\varepsilon \le d(\drive(w,s),\drive(w,t)) \le k^{|w|} d(s,t) \le k^N D < \varepsilon$, a
contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Contractive finite agents always phase-lock]
On a finite metric state space, separation and boundedness are automatic, so any
per-stimulus $k$-contraction with $k<1$ yields genuine phase-lock, with all sufficiently
long words locking.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Contraction is strictly stronger than phase-lock}

\begin{theorem}[No contractive metric with a reversible stimulus]\label{thm:nocontract}
Let $S$ be finite and metric, and suppose some stimulus $i_0$ acts bijectively. If every
stimulus is a $k$-contraction with $0 \le k < 1$, then $S$ is a single point.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The map $s \mapsto \delta(s,i_0)$ is a permutation of the finite set $S$; let $m>0$ be its
order, so its $m$-th iterate is the identity. Driving by $i_0^m$ fixes every state, while
Theorem \ref{thm:contract} gives $d(s,t) \le k^m d(s,t)$ with $k^m<1$, forcing
$d(s,t) = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Separation of mechanism from phenomenon]
The three-state \v{C}ern\'y agent phase-locks, yet its rotation stimulus is bijective, so
no metric on its state space makes every stimulus a uniform contraction. The analytic
mechanism is strictly stronger than the combinatorial phenomenon it explains.
\end{corollary}

\section{A structural calculus of phase-locking designs}\label{sec:structure}

\subsection{Parallel composition}

\begin{definition}
Given agents $\delta_1$ on $S_1$ and $\delta_2$ on $S_2$ over the same alphabet, their
parallel composition is
$(\delta_1 \parallel \delta_2)((p_1,p_2),i) = (\delta_1(p_1,i), \delta_2(p_2,i))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Compositional telepathy; locking times add]\label{thm:par}
If $w_1$ locks $\delta_1$ and $w_2$ locks $\delta_2$, then $w_1 w_2$ locks
$\delta_1 \parallel \delta_2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Drive factors componentwise. In the first component $w_1w_2$ locks by Lemma
\ref{lem:suffix}; in the second by Lemma \ref{lem:prefix}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Coarse-graining}

\begin{definition}[Simulation]
A map $f : S \to S'$ is a \emph{simulation} from $\delta$ to $\delta'$ if
$f(\delta(s,i)) = \delta'(f(s), i)$ for all $s,i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Coarse-graining preserves telepathy]
If $f$ is a surjective simulation and $w$ locks $\delta$, then $w$ locks $\delta'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Given $a,b \in S'$, lift to $s,t$ with $f(s)=a$, $f(t)=b$. Simulation gives
$\drive_{\delta'}(w, f(s)) = f(\drive_\delta(w,s))$, likewise for $t$; apply $f$ to
$\drive_\delta(w,s)=\drive_\delta(w,t)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Functoriality in the stimulus alphabet}

\begin{definition}[Relabelling]
Given $g : I' \to I$, the relabelled agent is $\delta^{g}(s,i') := \delta(s, g(i'))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
$w \in (I')^{*}$ locks $\delta^{g}$ if and only if $g(w) \in I^{*}$ locks $\delta$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Order rigidity}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone drive]
If $S$ is a linear order and every stimulus acts monotonically, then $\drive(w,\cdot)$ is
monotone for every $w$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Order rigidity]\label{thm:order}
Let $S$ be a linearly ordered state space with least element $\bot$ and greatest element
$\top$, and suppose every stimulus acts monotonically. Then $w$ locks $\delta$ if and only
if $\drive(w,\bot) = \drive(w,\top)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) For any $x$, monotonicity gives
$\drive(w,\bot) \le \drive(w,x) \le \drive(w,\top)$; equality of the outer terms squeezes
$\drive(w,x)$ to their common value.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
A monotone agent is phase-locking if and only if the single pair $(\bot,\top)$ is
mergeable.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Quadratic phase-lock time for monotone agents]
A monotone agent on a finite linearly ordered state space that phase-locks at all does so
within $|S|^2$ stimuli, improving the general cubic bound of Theorem \ref{thm:cubic}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
It suffices to merge $(\bot,\top)$, which by Corollary \ref{cor:short} takes at most
$|S|^2$ stimuli; Theorem \ref{thm:order} upgrades this to a lock.
\end{proof}

Order squeezes the entire state space between the images of the two extremes, so one merge
does the work of $|S|-1$ merges and the greedy loop collapses to a single step.

\section{Effectivity and extremal experiments}\label{sec:effective}

\subsection{Decidability}

\begin{theorem}[Reduction to finite search]
For finite $S$ and finite $I$, $\delta$ is phase-locking if and only if some word $w$ with
$|w| \le (|S|-1)|S|^2$ locks $\delta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Phase-lockability is decidable]
Whether two identical copies of a given finite agent design can be phase-locked is an
algorithmically decidable property of the design.
\end{corollary}

Naive search is exponential; the practical algorithm exploits Theorem \ref{thm:pairwise}
directly, computing merging words pairwise by breadth-first search in the pair space
$S\times S$ (with $n^2$ nodes) and then greedily concatenating, as in Lemma
\ref{lem:greedy}. This runs in time polynomial in $n$ and $|I|$ and returns a locking word
of length $O(n^3)$ when one exists.

\subsection{Exact extremal values}

For each $n \ge 2$, the \v{C}ern\'y agent $C_n$ has states $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$, alphabet
$\{a,b\}$, with $a : s \mapsto s+1 \bmod n$ and $b : 0 \mapsto 1$, $s \mapsto s$ for
$s \ne 0$.

\begin{theorem}[Three-state extremal value]\label{thm:cerny3}
For $C_3$ the word $baab$ locks, and no word of length $< 4$ locks. The minimal phase-lock
time is exactly $4 = (3-1)^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Four-state extremal value]
For $C_4$ the word $baaabaaab$ locks, and no word of length $< 9$ locks. The minimal
phase-lock time is exactly $9 = (4-1)^2$.
\end{theorem}

Both are established by certified exhaustive search over all shorter words, giving exact
data points $(n, \text{minimal lock time}) = (3,4), (4,9)$ on the quadratic pattern
$(n-1)^2$, far below the cubic bound of Theorem \ref{thm:cubic} (which gives $18$ and
$48$). Direct breadth-first search in the subset lattice extends the pattern: the minimal
locking word of $C_n$ has length exactly $(n-1)^2$ for every $n$ from $2$ through $12$.

\subsection{The \v{C}ern\'y conjecture in agent language}

\begin{conjecture}[\v{C}ern\'y, 1964; agent form]
Every finite phase-locking agent admits a locking word of length at most $(|S|-1)^2$.
\end{conjecture}

The theory above proves the cubic bound $(n-1)n^2$; the conjecture asserts $(n-1)^2$; the
\v{C}ern\'y agents show $(n-1)^2$ cannot be improved. The cubic bound factors as
\[
  \underbrace{(n-1)}_{\text{number of greedy merge steps}} \times
  \underbrace{n^2}_{\text{worst-case cost of one merge}},
\]
and both factors are simultaneously extremal only if the pair system possesses a ``longest
shortest merge'' structure that the image-collapsing process must repeatedly rebuild. In
the \v{C}ern\'y agents they are emphatically not simultaneously extremal: each merge costs
only about $n$, because after each collapse the surviving image is already well positioned
for the next. A potential function on the lattice of reachable image sets capturing this
amortization is the natural line of attack.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Deciding phase-lockability and constructing a locking word.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Pairwise merge table.} Build the pair system on $S\times S$ with transitions
$(s,t) \xrightarrow{i} (\delta(s,i),\delta(t,i))$ and run a backwards breadth-first search
from the diagonal, yielding for each pair a shortest merging word or a proof that none
exists.
\item \textbf{Certificate of failure.} If some pair cannot reach the diagonal, the design
is not phase-locking by Theorem \ref{thm:pairwise}. Halt.
\item \textbf{Greedy collapse.} Set $A \leftarrow S$, $w \leftarrow \varepsilon$. While
$|A| > 1$: pick distinct $s,t \in A$, look up their shortest merging word $v$, set
$w \leftarrow wv$ and $A \leftarrow \drive(v, A)$.
\item \textbf{Return} $w$.
\end{enumerate}

Step 1 costs $O(|I|\,|S|^2)$ time and $O(|S|^2)$ space; step 3 runs at most $|S|-1$ times,
each appending a word of length $\le |S|^2$. Total time $O(|I||S|^2 + |S|^4)$, output
length $O(|S|^3)$, matching Theorem \ref{thm:cubic}.

\paragraph{Exact minimal phase-lock time.}
Breadth-first search over the \emph{subset space} $2^S$, starting at $S$ and seeking a
singleton, returns the exact minimum. This is exponential in $|S|$ --- unavoidably so,
since computing the minimal locking word length is NP-hard --- but practical for
$|S| \le 20$.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Resetting without sensing.}
Stripped of the doppelg\"anger framing, the subject is the theory of blind resetting. A
device has drifted into an unknown internal configuration with no sensor to read it; is
there a fixed command sequence returning it to a known configuration whatever its current
state? That is a locking word. Manifestations include part orienters aligning components
on a vibrating tray by an open-loop shake sequence, self-synchronizing codes recovering
frame alignment after corruption, robotic recovery routines, and homing sequences in
conformance testing.

\paragraph{The physics reading.}
Theorem \ref{thm:rev} is the discrete shadow of a familiar principle: reversible dynamics
cannot bring two differently-initialized copies of a system into agreement, because it
never reduces the number of distinguishable configurations. Rank is a count of surviving
distinguishability and Theorem \ref{thm:rank} says it decreases monotonically --- an
entropy-like statement. Theorem \ref{thm:contract} is the quantitative metric version, and
Theorem \ref{thm:nocontract} closes the circle.

\paragraph{The no-signalling reading.}
Correlation between the two agents, however perfect, is never a channel (Theorem
\ref{thm:nosig}): each agent's trajectory is a function of its own inputs and its own
initial state. Theorem \ref{thm:distinct} sharpens this: remove the shared cause and the
correlation vanishes.

\paragraph{The two regimes.}
In the \emph{analytic (contractive) regime}, every stimulus shrinks distances; locking is
automatic, uniform in the stream, and all sufficiently long words lock. In the
\emph{combinatorial (\v{C}ern\'y) regime}, some stimuli are reversible; locking requires a
strategy that uses reversible stimuli to position states for the rare dissipative ones,
and only special words lock. Theorem \ref{thm:nocontract} proves the second regime
strictly larger, and almost all the difficulty of the extremal question resides in it.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{C1 --- Quadratic phase-lock time (\v{C}ern\'y conjecture, agent form).}
For every finite agent that phase-locks, is there a locking word of length at most
$(|S|-1)^2$? We proved the cubic bound $(|S|-1)|S|^2$ and verified the quadratic value
exactly on the \v{C}ern\'y agents with three and four states. This is the classical
\v{C}ern\'y conjecture, open since 1964. The key insight is that our cubic bound
decomposes as (number of greedy merge steps) $\times$ (cost of one pairwise merge), and
both factors are simultaneously extremal only if the pair system has a Hamiltonian-like
shortest-merge structure that the image-collapsing process must repeatedly rebuild --- a
tension that a potential function on the lattice of reachable image sets should be able to
exploit. With rank, the ideal structure of locking words, and a certified exhaustive
search procedure available, one can systematically test candidate potential functions on
all agents up to five or six states before attempting a general proof; the greedy-collapse
proof is written so that only the ``cost of one merge step'' lemma need be replaced.

\paragraph{C2 --- Order rigidity beyond linear orders.}
Let $S$ be a finite lattice and let every stimulus act as a lattice homomorphism. Is
phase-lock then equivalent to the mergeability of $\bot$ and $\top$, with lock time at
most $\mathrm{height}(S)\cdot|S|$ rather than $|S|^3$? We proved this for linear orders and
monotone (not necessarily homomorphic) transitions, with a quadratic bound. The lattice
case is open: the monotone squeezing argument uses linearity only through antisymmetric
trapping between $\bot$ and $\top$, whereas a genuine lattice needs a chain-decomposition
argument. Since the order-rigidity proof is short once monotonicity of drive is available,
the lattice generalization is a low-risk, high-reward extension that would also sharpen
the composition bound.

\paragraph{Further avenues.}
(i) \emph{Probabilistic environments}: replace uniform counting with general stationary or
Markovian stimulus sources and determine the exact locking-time distribution.
(ii) \emph{Approximate phase-lock}: quantify partial synchronization by the rank profile of
a random word, obtaining a rank-descent curve interpolating between $|S|$ and $1$.
(iii) \emph{Noisy channels}: allow the two agents to observe slightly different streams and
determine the maximal discrepancy rate consistent with recurrent phase-lock.
(iv) \emph{Continuous state spaces}: identify the correct generalization of rank for
measurable dynamics and connect to synchronization by common noise.
(v) \emph{Compositional bounds}: determine when the additive bound of Theorem \ref{thm:par}
is tight.

\section{Conclusion}

Two identical machines, separated in space, unable to communicate, and started in unknown
and differing configurations, can be driven into exactly the same internal state by a
common environmental signal. We have characterized when this is possible, how long it
takes, how typical it is, what mechanism drives it, how it behaves under design
operations, and --- most importantly --- what it cannot do: it is impossible for
reversible agents, it fails without finiteness, it fails without a shared stimulus stream,
and it never constitutes a communication channel. What looks like telepathy is two
identical mechanisms forgetting their differences at the same rate, in the same way,
because the world told them the same story.

